{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Whoever gives of his seed to Molech — he shall surely be put to death; the people of the land shall stone him with stones.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted <b>אשר יתן מזרעו</b> — 'whoever gives of his seed' — to mean: if he gave some of his seed he is liable; if all of his seed he is exempt. And its explanation is: exempt from stoning, for when he gives some of his seed he is liable to stoning and he has atonement through that death, since he received his sentence in this world — for everyone who is liable to one of the four deaths of the court confesses and is atoned, and merits the life of the world to come. And so is the way of all who are executed: they confess, as we find with Achan, who was liable to the court's death and confessed, and Joshua said to him (Joshua 7): ״יעכרך HaShem ביום הזה״ — 'May HaShem trouble you this day' — and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: 'this day you are troubled, but you are not troubled for the world to come.' But this one who was so wicked and sunk so deep in corruption as to give all his seed to Molech — he is exempt from the court's death, and he does not confess and is never atoned; rather, his punishment is bound and stored for something worse than this, in the hands of Heaven, in the judgments of Gehinnom and his torments in one of its chambers. For the sin is inscribed in his soul and fixed in it, in the manner of (Numbers 15): ״הכרת תכרת הנפש ההיא עונה בה״ — 'that soul shall be utterly cut off, its iniquity is in it' — as stated regarding the blasphemer: his body is cut off and his soul is cut off from the world to come. <br>And the sense of <b>מות יומת</b> — 'he shall surely be put to death' — is by the court; <b>עם הארץ ירגמוהו באבן</b> — 'the people of the land shall stone him' — if the court has no power [to carry out the sentence]. And all this is with witnesses and warning. And if there are no witnesses, <b>ואני אתן את פני באיש ההוא והכרתי אותו</b> — 'and I will set My face against that man and cut him off' — if he did so privately and there are no witnesses in the matter. And if there are witnesses but the people of the land conceal it and close their eyes to him out of fear of the family members, so as not to put him to death — behold, the Holy One, blessed be He, will cut off him and all who agree with him and those who cover for him. And therefore his family is mentioned within the punishment, as it says: ״ושמתי אני את פני באיש ההוא ובמשפחתו״ — 'and I will set My face against that man and against his family.'" "<b>And the Rav</b>, of blessed memory, wrote in the Guide for the Perplexed regarding the reason for Molech: that the fire-worshippers in those times would devise strategems to strengthen their faith, and they would proclaim that whoever did not pass his son and daughter through the fire — his sons would die. And there is no doubt that each and every one would strive to do so, because of his great tenderness and fear for his son, and to minimize the son's harm [and his disgrace] in that it was only passing him over the fire. And all the more so did they strive in this, since the matter of young children was entrusted to women, and women's understanding is light upon them and their reason is weak to believe anything. And because of this the Torah set itself against this practice most forcefully and enacted against it the strongest measures that it did not enact against other kinds of idolatry. And this is what is said: ״למען טמא את מקדשי״ — 'to defile My sanctuary.' And Scripture informs you concerning one who does this that the Holy One, blessed be He, will cut off his seed; this is what it says: ״ושמתי אני את פני באיש ההוא ובמשפחתו והכרתי אותו״ — 'and I will set My face against that man and against his family and cut him off' — for corresponding to what he intended in this deed, that his sons would live and his seed would endure, He informed us that He will destroy and cut off his seed — measure for measure. End of quotation. And I have already written of this at the verse (Leviticus 18): ״ומזרעך לא תתן״ — 'and of your seed you shall not give' — for the opinion of some of the commentators is that the child would be burned by passing it through fire many times until it died. But such is not the opinion of the Rav here, for it was nothing but passing [through fire] alone. And according to his view, what is written (Deuteronomy 13): ״כי גם את בניהם ואת בנותיהם ישרפו באש לאלהיהם״ — 'for even their sons and their daughters they burn in the fire to their gods' — is not Molech but another idolatry."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:27:29.908372151Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}